WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
I want to start with laying out an example

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.980
cloud-native application that I've architected

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:08.420
and I know how to build it out.

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:11.180
So, let's start with the front-end.

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:14.000
We'll call this the UI portion here.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.920
Below that we've got the BFF ("Back-end For Front-end").

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:23.400
So, this is serving the API's for that UI to serve up information.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.600
So, the UI accesses the BFF

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:29.080
and that, in turn, is going to access

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:32.460
the microservice or the back-end layer.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:36.720
So, in here let's say "back end".

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.180
Now, obviously for higher value services -

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:40.500
let's say that back-end

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:45.280
goes out to something like AI capabilities,

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:49.100
and in addition, maybe a database.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:52.100
So, Matt as the expert, I'm going to hand this off to you.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:54.740
This is the application architecture that I want.

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:58.880
How do I start migrating this over to a cloud-native approach

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:01.020
and what are the DevOps considerations

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:02.360
that I need to take into account?

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:05.560
Ok. So, you've already laid out some of the separation of concerns.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:10.003
You've got a component that is focused on delivering a user experience,

00:01:10.003 --> 00:01:13.300
which, again, can be  containerized and packaged.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:17.000
You've then maybe got a back-end for front-end which is serving

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.940
UI-friendly APIs

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:22.500
and abstracting and orchestrating across a number of back-end.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.820
So, you've got your 3 logical points.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:28.860
So, moving forward, what you typically do is take

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:32.560
this component and start to break it into a pipeline

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:35.000
that will enable you to offer some discipline

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.340
around how you build, deploy, and test.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:42.280
So, what we typically do here is we're going to use DevOps

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:45.480
and we're going to create a pipeline,

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:48.560
and this pipeline is going to consist of a number of stages

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.120
that will take us through

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:53.100
the lifecycle of building and packaging this component.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:56.660
So, typically the first step is to clone the code

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:59.320
from your source code management, which is typically Git

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:02.460
or some kind of Git-based technology, GitHub, GitLab,

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:05.300
and then the next step is to build the app.

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:08.760
So, "Build App".

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:12.140
In this portion, when you're actually building out the application,

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:15.100
you have considerations for a Node.js app,

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:16.640
you have things like NPM,

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:19.340
Java, you have to figure out the build
process for that.

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:22.080
So, the pipeline is kind of configured

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:24.000
to build each one of these components

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.700
based on the programming language?

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:29.560
Right. So, typically you have one pipeline per component

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:31.400
and, as you correctly stated,

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:34.300
if you're building a UI and it's got React in it,

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:37.820
you're going to use a web pack to build the UI TypeScript code,

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:39.440
package that into a form

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:41.940
that will then be package-ready for run.

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:43.015
So, there are steps

00:02:43.015 --> 00:02:44.780
- and, again, with a Spring app,

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:48.160
a Spring Boot app, that you'll package it using Maven or Gradle,

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:52.570
and we know that Node.js you'd use NPM and various other steps.

00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:54.880
So, this part of the pipeline

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:58.820
is about packaging the source code in the way that it's needed

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:00.360
to then be run.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:05.680
But then, typically, at this point the next step is to to run a set of tests.

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:09.080
So, you run a set of unit tests against the code,

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:10.780
you validate code coverage.

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:13.160
And then this enables you to determine

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:16.740
whether any code changes that have been made in the pipeline are valid.

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:20.720
And again, these steps are sequentially moving along,

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:23.640
but if any one of these fails it will stop the build,

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:25.920
you'll be informed as if as a developer

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:28.020
and then you'll go back and fix the code

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:29.500
or fix the test.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:32.740
So, just to clarify at this level we're going to do

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:36.520
unit tests, so tests within kind of the app context.

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:39.220
Not really considering

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:41.620
connections between the different components.

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:44.340
Yeah. Today we're not going to cover that the integration story

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:46.020
or performance testing,

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:49.020
but typically when you're building a pipeline you need to

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:51.120
test the code that you've written

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:53.660
using various techniques.

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:58.240
Typically, you can use test-driven development which is a concept we use in the Garage.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:02.180
So, you write the test first and then create the code to validate that.

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:03.620
You can use other frameworks,

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:07.780
most of the major programming models have good test frameworks around them,

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:11.780
whether it's Java, Node, or other languages.

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:13.100
So, next step:

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:15.980
again, one of the key things to try and drive for

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:18.460
is to get to a point of continuous delivery.

00:04:18.460 --> 00:04:21.100
This is a continuous integration pipeline,

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:24.160
but if you fail the test then that's going to prevent

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:27.680
this package of code moving into a test environment.

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:32.000
So, another common technique we use is code scanning,

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:35.280
or vulnerability scanning, or security scanning.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:38.680
So, what we do here is we're looking for vulnerabilities,

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:40.420
we're looking for test coverage,

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:42.300
we're looking for quality gates.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:44.580
So, if your code isn't a good enough quality,

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:47.740
from a code analysis perspective,

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:50.680
we could actually stop the build and say we're not going to move

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:53.497
this microservice further along the build process.

00:04:53.497 --> 00:04:56.320
Right. So, if we were building out this

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:59.480
- let's say the BFF application was

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:03.880
a container-based application running in IKS (IBM Cloud Kubernetes Service),

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:08.220
We have some capabilities to allow you to test for that scanning, right?

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:10.220
It's the Vulnerability Advisor.

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:12.280
So, would that exist in this phase then?

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:13.760
So, you tested  the code, then you...

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:17.960
Yeah. Again, I'm lumping in one or two different stages here,

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:21.640
you can do vulnerability scan, you can do code scan,

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:24.380
it's kind of a  common technique to make sure.

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:28.220
The good thing about vulnerability scanning is you're validating

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:30.960
that there's no security holes in the Docker image,

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:32.860
or the container image as you build it.

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:33.800
Got it. OK.

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:36.960
So, now that we've got up to the scanning phase,

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:39.120
what's our next phase - where are we going?

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:40.420
The next step is

00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:43.740
to take the application that we built and tested and scanned,

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:47.740
and now we're gonna build it into an image.

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:51.380
So, we call it a "build image".

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:54.240
So, what this is doing is using the tools

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:59.440
to to package up the code that we built and put it inside a container.

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:01.720
And once we've built the image

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:06.020
we then store that image out in an image registry

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:07.760
with a tagged version that goes with it.

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:10.720
Right. So, I guess I got ahead of that right there

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:13.300
- so, that's where we would actually do that vulnerability scanning:

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:18.240
once we've tested the code itself, done some some scanning at that level,

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:21.660
once we build the image then, something like vulnerability advisors ...

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:24.120
Right. So, you could have that as another stage,

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:27.760
but, again, if the vulnerability is poor

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:30.300
then you could prevent this moving forward

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:32.300
and that will inform the developers

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:35.720
to either upgrade the level of base images they're using

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:38.780
or fix a number of the packages that they've included in it.

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:40.720
So, basically every step of the way

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:44.420
- if anything fails you're notified of that

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:47.100
and you can go back and fix that.

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:52.780
Right - and at the next stage, now you have an image, and the next thing is to deploy it.

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:58.860
So, what we're  looking to do is to take that image and deploy it inside an OpenShift managed platform

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:02.650
so it will move the container from the image registry and deploy it.

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:05.900
And there are a number of different techniques for deployment that's are used.

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:08.220
Some developers are using Helm,

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:10.940
but the more modern approach is to use operators,

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:13.960
so there's a life cycle around that component when it gets deployed.

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:15.680
So, and then this deploy -

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:18.640
let's say I have a a Kubernetes environment -

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:22.880
so you would deploy an application,

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:24.740
let's say the BFF application,

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:27.260
into that Kubernetes environment, right?. Yep.

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:31.780
OK, and I'm guessing at this phase this is still part of the developer flow,

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:35.960
- would this be the development environment that you're pushing into, or the test environment?

00:07:35.960 --> 00:07:40.320
So, typically a continuous integration flow

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:44.400
builds and packages the code up for the development environment.

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:47.520
When we talk in a few seconds we'll more talk a bit more about

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:51.340
how we move that package of code from the container registry

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:53.240
out into a test environment.

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:56.980
Got it, so right here, like that. Yep.

00:07:56.980 --> 00:08:00.220
So, the final step is to validate the health.

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:03.260
So, what you're really asking here is,

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:05.820
"Is the container running?"

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:09.120
- is it sending back operational information

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:11.560
such that you can determine that it's healthy enough

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:14.540
to validate that, not only that the tests have run,

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:16.360
but actually it started,

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:18.740
and it's communicating with its dependent services,

00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:22.440
and it's going to operate in the way that you'd expect it to.

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:25.780
Of course, yeah. So, this is where you

00:08:25.780 --> 00:08:27.920
connect it up to the different components

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:30.340
and make sure they're all working together seamlessly.

00:08:30.340 --> 00:08:32.720
This is where you would probably

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:34.880
find issues with integration,

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:38.140
or how the teams are connecting up with each other,

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:40.060
API contracts, and those kind of things,

00:08:40.060 --> 00:08:42.040
those issues will start to bubble up in this space.

00:08:42.040 --> 00:08:44.400
Yes, and again, the health input is important

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:47.820
because you can hook that into operational tools

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:50.280
like Sysdig and LogDNA and

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:52.500
other monitoring that will give you

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:56.580
a better feel of the current state of your applications as they run.

00:08:56.580 --> 00:08:59.940
So, this has got us as far through the development cycle.

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:02.700
The next step is to -

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:06.171
and, again, introduce - this is starting to be common in the industry,

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:08.780
is to use a technique called GitOps

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:10.120
where you would now say

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:13.930
I've got my application, I built it, I packaged it, I've tested it,

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:15.340
I've validated it.

00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:18.406
What I'm now going to do is update a Git repo

00:09:18.406 --> 00:09:21.600
with the build number, the tagged version,

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:25.600
and the reference point to the image registry.

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:29.780
And then GitOps can then trigger off a deployment of that image

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:32.560
out into a test environment

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:34.642
with all the other components that go with it,

00:09:34.642 --> 00:09:37.740
and there are  a number of GitOps tools out in the market

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:40.940
and one of the ones we use in the Garage is Argo CD,

00:09:40.940 --> 00:09:45.060
which allows you to monitor a webhook of a Git repo

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:46.740
and then it will pull the image,

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:49.700
it will pull the deployment reference, and then package it and deploy it

00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:52.840
ready for use in testing.

00:09:52.840 --> 00:09:56.100
So, basically the same quality that developers have been doing

00:09:56.100 --> 00:10:00.260
forever with SCMs to manage different versions of their code,

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:03.760
now operations team are taking advantage of that same approach

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:07.200
to basically operationalize the deployment

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:10.020
of these  actual images, containers, applications.

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:12.480
Absolutely, and it comes back to a point we made earlier,

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:15.220
that this is about discipline and repeatability.

00:10:15.220 --> 00:10:19.020
There's no humans hurt in this process
as you go through it,

00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:22.120
and the less humans  touching these steps the better.

00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:24.900
Again, one of the things we often do with clients is

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:25.920
we'll work with them

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:28.740
and we'll discover that there's some human process in the middle

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:32.140
and that really slows down your ability to execute.

00:10:32.140 --> 00:10:35.840
So, it's about automation, discipline, and repeatability,

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:37.760
and if you can get to this point

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:40.840
and prove that this code is good enough to run in production,

00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:44.260
you can then start to move towards

00:10:44.260 --> 00:10:48.720
that golden milestone of delivering continuous delivery.

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:51.340
Right. So, once you've automated all of this,

00:10:51.340 --> 00:10:54.980
that's when you can truly say you have CI/CD.

00:10:54.980 --> 00:10:57.580
That's that's when you can finally get to that level.

